# Lab 4: Docker image optimization and export/load

In this lab I built an image from the existing Dockerfile (image1), then wrote a multi-stage Dockerfile that runs as a non-root user (image2), compared the sizes, and then saved/loaded image2 as a tar file.

App source: https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/ImageOptimization-.git

## Clone the code

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/ImageOptimization-.git
cd ImageOptimization-
```

## Step 1: Build image1 from the existing Dockerfile

```bash
docker build -t image1 .
docker images image1
```

Size of image1: **<write the size here>**

## Step 2: Run container1 and verify

```bash
docker run -d --name container1 -p 8080:8080 image1
docker ps
docker logs container1
```


Then stop and delete it:

```bash
docker stop container1
docker rm container1
```

## Step 3: Multi-stage Dockerfile with a non-root user

I replaced the Dockerfile with a multi-stage one. The first stage builds the app and the second stage only has what is needed to run it.

```dockerfile
# stage 1: build
FROM maven:3.9.15-eclipse-temurin-17-alpine AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY pom.xml . 
COPY src ./src
RUN mvn package

# stage 2: run
```
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre
WORKDIR /app
RUN useradd -m appuser
COPY --from=build --chown=appuser:appuser /app/target/demo-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar app.jar
USER appuser
EXPOSE 8080
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]


## Step 4: Build image2 and compare sizes

```bash
docker build -t image2 .
docker images
```

| Image | Size |
|-------|------|
| image1 | 667 MB |
| image2 | 461 MB |

image2 is smaller because the final image doesn't have the build tools and the extra files that were only needed to build the app.

## Step 5: Run container2 and check it is non-root

```bash
docker run -d --name container2 -p 8082:8080 image2
docker logs container2
docker exec container2 whoami
```

The output of `whoami` should be `appuser` (not `root`).

Stop and delete it:

```bash
docker stop container2
docker rm container2
```

## Step 6: Save image2 as a tar file

```bash
docker save -o lab-image.tar image2
ls -lh lab-image.tar
```

## Step 7: Delete image2 and load it again

```bash
docker rmi image2
docker images            # image2 is gone

docker load -i lab-image.tar
docker images            # image2 is back
```

